spec:
  grid: 10-14 rows x 10-14 cols, always square [observed], always even
  background: color 0
  divider:
    color: 8 [observed]; must be the unique most-common non-bg color
    shape: L-shaped border, 2 cells thick, fills legend-rows x data-cols AND data-rows x legend-cols
  legend:
    size: 2x2, sits in one of four grid corners
    colors: 4 distinct values, all != bg and != divider
    data_color: one of the 4 legend colors also serves as the single foreground color in the data region
  data_region:
    position: opposite corner from legend; occupies (grid_dim - 2) x (grid_dim - 2) cells
    content: bg + exactly one foreground color (= data_color from legend)
    dimensions: even x even so it splits into 4 equal quadrants
    pixel_density: 6-25% of data cells are foreground [observed]
    quadrants: each quadrant has >= 1 foreground pixel
  transform: for each quadrant (top-left, top-right, bottom-left, bottom-right), replace every data-colored pixel with the corresponding legend cell color; all other pixels unchanged

generation_strategy:
  approach: direct construction (constraints are simple enough for forward generation)
  steps:
    1. Pick grid size N from {10, 12, 14} (even, square).
    2. Pick a corner for the legend (one of 4).
    3. Choose 4 distinct colors from 1-7,9 (avoiding bg=0 and divider=8) for legend cells.
    4. Designate one of the 4 legend colors as data_color.
    5. Fill the (N-2)x(N-2) data region with bg; for each of the 4 quadrants, randomly place 1-5 data_color pixels (~8-20% density per quadrant).
    6. Fill the L-shaped divider border (2 rows x full width + 2 cols x full height, minus 2x2 legend corner) with divider color 8.
    7. Place the 2x2 legend in the chosen corner.
    8. Build output by copying input, then recoloring each quadrant's data pixels to the matching legend color.
